  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Cold wet conditions today and a winter storm for the front range\n  and southwest Montana. \n\n- A quick rebound expected with warm temperatures again by late\n  week. \n\nA cold front is moving across the region this morning, and by this\nevening the snow levels will be low enough for snow reach many\nvalleys in west central, northwest, and southwest Montana.\nNortheasterly flow along the Continental Divide will be enhanced\nby the terrain allowing much higher snow rates there. Snow amounts\nat Rogers, MacDonald, and Homestake passes may exceed 4-6 inches\nby Monday morning. To complicate things, it has recently been very\nwarm and the ground is still holding some of that heat. So when it\ndoes start snowing, most of it will melt at first, at least on\npaved surfaces. But after the sun goes down, or where the snow\nrates are high enough, the snow will eventually overcome that. At\nfirst paved surfaces will be wet, then slushy, and over some\npasses it will be snow on top of a slushy base. Taken along with\nthe winds, these conditions are perfect for hypothermia, so if\nyou're in the back-country, be prepared wet, snowy, cold, and\nwindy conditions. \n\nHigh resolution models are also showing the potential for east\nwind gusts over 40 mph on the southeast end of Flathead Lake. This\npattern has been known to occur before, but models usually under\nforecast the winds or miss it completely, so its very interesting\nto see this. Between that effect and the gap winds through Bad\nRock Canyon, a wind advisory has been issued this evening through\nSunday morning for portions of Flathead and Lake counties in\nMontana. \n\nSunday or Monday will be the coldest day, but hard freezes are\nexpected in the valleys of western Montana through mid-week next\nweek. \n\nIn the long term, models show a ridge rebounding quickly next\nweek. By the end of the week, afternoon high temperatures are\nexpected to be in the 60s in western Montana and in the 70s at the\nlower elevations of central Idaho.",
